# REPORT — wai-e8tc

## Root cause (with file:line)

**No code defect exists in `wai add` step-tag resolution.** The reported lag could not be reproduced through the sequential CLI path on either the current build or the session-era installed binary (`851a176-main-dirty`):

- `src/commands/pipeline/orchestration.rs:353-359` (`cmd_next`) — persists the advanced `current_step` to `.wai/pipeline-runs/<run>.yml` **before** printing the new step prompt (`fs::write(&run_path, yaml)` at :358, `print_step` at :363-365). Any process that observes the new step prompt can rely on the yml being current.
- `src/commands/add.rs:554-567` (`resolve_current_step_id`) — reads the yml fresh from disk on every invocation (no cache); indexes `definition.steps.get(run.current_step)` — same 0-based indexing as `wai pipeline current` (`src/commands/pipeline/queries.rs:303-307`). No off-by-one.
- `src/config.rs:441-447` (`resolve_active_pipeline_run`) — env var then `.last-run` pointer; both point at the same run id in all observed evidence (artifact tags carried the **correct** run ids, ruling out hypothesis (c) pointer staleness).

**Hypotheses verdict:** (a) stale yml — refuted (write precedes prompt; reads are uncached); (b) off-by-one — refuted (identical indexing in add and current); (c) stale `.last-run` — refuted (run ids in evidence tags are correct).

**Most parsimonious explanation of the 4× observations — operator sequencing:** the artifact was added *before* the advancing `wai pipeline next`. Tags are immutable at add time, so an artifact added at step N is tagged step N even after the run advances to N+1. The evidence pattern (`gates-*` research tagged `pipeline-step:claim` while yml said `current_step: 1`) matches exactly. Compounding factor: when the gate then blocks, `report_gate_failures` → `return Ok(())` (`orchestration.rs:342-346`) makes a **blocked advance exit 0** — a scripted/agent flow can miss the failed advance entirely. (Fixing that exit-code semantics is gate-behavior territory → follow-up candidate under epic `wai-fvhv`, per the brief's follow-up threshold.)

